[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Design Patterns in Unity: Dependency Injection, Service Locator, and Observer"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As Unity projects grow in scale and complexity, maintaining clean, decoupled, and testable code becomes a significant challenge. Adopting proven software architecture design patterns helps manage dependencies and communication across systems. This tutorial explores three essential design patterns commonly used in Unity development: Dependency Injection, the Service Locator, and the Observer pattern."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Observer Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern that defines a one-to-many dependency between objects. When one object (the subject) changes its state, all its dependents (observers) are notified automatically. In Unity, this is exceptionally useful for decoupling UI systems from game logic."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerHealth : MonoBehaviour
{
    public event Action<int> OnHealthChanged;
    [SerializeField] private int currentHealth = 100;

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        currentHealth -= amount;
        currentHealth = Mathf.Max(currentHealth, 0);

        // Notify all subscribers
        OnHealthChanged?.Invoke(currentHealth);
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the PlayerHealth component does not need a direct reference to any UI script. It simply invokes the event. The HealthUI component can subscribe to this event independently."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UI;

public class HealthUI : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private PlayerHealth playerHealth;
    [SerializeField] private Text healthText;

    private void OnEnable()
    {
        playerHealth.OnHealthChanged += UpdateHealthDisplay;
    }

    private void OnDisable()
    {
        playerHealth.OnHealthChanged -= UpdateHealthDisplay;
    }

    private void UpdateHealthDisplay(int newHealth)
    {
        healthText.text = "Health: " + newHealth;
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Service Locator Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Service Locator pattern provides a central registry that acts as a global point of access for various services or managers (such as an AudioService, AnalyticsService, or SaveSystem) without requiring classes to hardcode their dependencies."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public static class ServiceLocator
{
    private static readonly Dictionary<Type, object> services = new Dictionary<Type, object>();

    public static void Register<T>(T service)
    {
        var type = typeof(T);
        if (!services.ContainsKey(type))
        {
            services[type] = service;
        }
    }

    public static T Get<T>()
    {
        var type = typeof(T);
        if (services.ContainsKey(type))
        {
            return (T)services[type];
        }
        throw new Exception("Service not found: " + type.Name);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use the Service Locator, you register your services upon initialization (typically in a bootstrapper script) and retrieve them wherever needed."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Dependency Injection (DI)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection is a technique where objects receive their dependencies from an external source rather than creating them internally. In Unity, manual constructor injection is tricky because MonoBehaviours are instantiated by the engine. However, method injection or frameworks like Zenject/Extenject are heavily utilized."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public interface IScoreService
{
    void AddScore(int amount);
}

public class ScoreService : IScoreService
{
    public void AddScore(int amount)
    {
        // Logic to add score
    }
}

public class Enemy : MonoBehaviour
{
    private IScoreService scoreService;

    // Method Injection
    public void Construct(IScoreService scoreService)
    {
        this.scoreService = scoreService;
    }

    public void Die()
    {
        scoreService?.AddScore(10);
        Destroy(gameObject);
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Observer: Best for event-driven communication and decoupling UI from game logic."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Service Locator: Best for global cross-cutting concerns, though it can obscure hidden dependencies if overused."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dependency Injection: Best for strict modularity, testability, and explicit dependency management."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By intelligently combining these patterns in your Unity projects, you will achieve clean, maintainable, and scalable game architecture."}]
